<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Two years of gradual, painless blurring of vision in both eyes, worse on the right, with glare and trouble recognizing faces, in a 70-year-old retired backcountry guide who has had one fall at dusk and has stopped driving at night. Please establish the cause, exclude glaucoma, macular degeneration and diabetic eye disease, and advise on surgery. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two to three weeks. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the cause of the vision loss, and are glaucoma, macular degeneration and diabetic retinopathy excluded?</item><item>Is surgery indicated, and on which eye first?</item><item>What lens implant and timing do you advise for the two eyes, and is same-day surgery on both eyes an option?</item><item>What drops and postoperative visits will he need, and how can he manage them without reading?</item><item>What should we advise about falls and driving before, between and after the operations?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mr. Bridger, 70, a retired backcountry guide, is seen at the request of Dr. Okafor-Westbrook for two years of gradual, painless blurring of vision in both eyes, worse on the right, with glare, faded colors and difficulty recognizing faces; he has had one fall at dusk and has stopped driving at night. No flashes, floaters, pain or redness; no diabetes, steroid use, eye injury or previous eye surgery. He does not read; he wants to see faces, the ground and the road. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>James Bridger was born on March 17, 1804, in Richmond, Virginia, the son of James and Chloe Bridger. The family moved west to Missouri when he was a boy, and his mother, a brother and his father died there within a few years, leaving him an orphan at about fourteen with a younger sister. [D]</item><item>After an apprenticeship as a blacksmith, he joined William Ashley&apos;s fur-trapping expedition to the Rocky Mountains in 1822. He spent more than four decades in the West as a trapper, a partner in a fur company, a trader, and from 1849 to 1868 a guide and scout for emigrants, surveyors and the Army. [D]</item><item>In a fight with Blackfeet men in 1832 he was struck in the back by two arrows; the iron head of one could not be removed and stayed deep in his back. [D]</item><item>In August 1835, at the fur traders&apos; rendezvous on the Green River, the missionary physician Marcus Whitman removed the arrowhead from his back. A companion of Whitman&apos;s described an iron arrowhead three inches long, hooked at the point where it had struck a large bone and surrounded by tissue that had grown around it; the operation was difficult, and Bridger bore it steadily while trappers and Indians watched. [D]</item><item>In 1843 he and his partner Louis Vasquez founded Fort Bridger, a trading post for emigrants on Blacks Fork of the Green River in what is now southwestern Wyoming. [D]</item><item>He married three Native women in succession: the daughter of a Flathead chief, who died in 1846; a Ute woman, who died in 1849; and in 1850 a Shoshone woman. He had several children, some of whom he sent to school in Missouri. [D]</item><item>He could not read, but he enjoyed having books read aloud to him; an army officer&apos;s account from his scouting years describes him listening to Shakespeare&apos;s plays read until midnight. [D]</item><item>By his early sixties the effects of arthritis, rheumatism and rugged frontier life had caught up with him; in 1867 at Fort Phil Kearny he was troubled by rheumatism that hampered his movements, and in 1868 he moved back to his farm near Westport, Missouri. [D]</item><item>In his later years he had a goiter and a rupture (hernia), which troubled him somewhat; the cause of the goiter is not recorded. [D]</item><item>His eyesight began to fail in the late 1860s, and from 1873 it failed rapidly, so that he could recognize friends only by the sound of their voices. The cause of his failing sight is not recorded in the sources read. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

